=== PARITY (vs web DrivingDynamicsWidget.tsx) ===
  Data sources -> bound via the shared state-holder seam (DrivingDynamicsSource -> DrivingDynamicsModel):
    useVehicles, useDrivingDynamics (GET /drives/dynamics?vehicle_id), useAccelerationDistribution
    (GET /drives/acceleration-distribution?vehicle_id), useTranslation, useThemeChartPalette. The production app
    wires DrivingDynamicsSource over the KMP DrivingStore (StateHolderModel<LoadableState<…>>); the view performs
    NO HTTP. No direct transport in the view.
  Projection -> DrivingDynamicsBuilder is a 1:1 port of the web useMemo/derived pipeline:
    deriveSeverity(avgAccel,avgBrake) = avg<0.15 calm / <0.3 normal / <0.5 sporty / else aggressive ;
    isSmooth(maxG)=maxG<0.4 ; gaugeColor(g) bands <0.2 success / <0.4 info / <0.6 warning / else danger ;
    maxG = max(maxAccelerationG,maxBrakingG,maxCorneringG) ; the 3 gauges read avgAccelerationG / avgBrakingG /
    maxCorneringG (lateral uses the PEAK cornering g — there is no avg cornering field) ; histogramData
    step = G_MAX(1.2)/values.count, label = fmtNumber(i*step,2), count = count ?? 0. Verified by the executed
    tests + the real-SDK runtime harness below (severity/tone boundaries, maxG, gauge mapping, step=0.4 → 0.00/0.40/0.80).
  Composition -> widget shell chrome (gauge.medium icon in accent + "Driving Dynamics" title + freshness chip +
    refresh + optional open) · COMPACT (cols<=1): large maxG readout + "Max g" + Smooth/Aggressive badge ·
    STANDARD: three RadialGauges (Accel / Brake / Lateral, color-banded, centered value, caption) + driving-style chip ·
    WIDE (cols>=3): + the acceleration-distribution histogram (tap-to-inspect tooltip, thinned axis) — all web branches.
  Shared components -> reuses Color.TS / Font.TS / TSSpacing / TSRadius / TSMotion / TSSkeleton / TSChartPalette
